{;; kmet's RFC 0014 provider lib (see README.md in this dir): the JDK
 ;; classes the jolt ecosystem does not supply that kmet's crypto/oauth/ADC
 ;; paths need — java.util.Base64 MIME decoding and the
 ;; java.net.http.HttpTimeoutException ctor. RSA is not on this list —
 ;; jolt.crypto provides it (EC and RSA), so the claims for
 ;; java.security.Signature / KeyPairGenerator / KeyFactory live in crypto's
 ;; own :jolt/provides (jolt allows a class only one provider).
 ;;
 ;; The native libs (libcrypto/libssl) and the crypto java.* classes come
 ;; from io.github.jolt-lang/crypto. This lib does NOT require it: crypto's
 ;; own :jolt/provides claims resolve its classes on the first reference,
 ;; whatever loaded first — RFC 0014 gives a class one provider, and a
 ;; declared provider owns the members it registers. jolt dedupes natives by
 ;; :name, so the app declaring crypto once is enough.
 ;;
 ;; Inert on bb/JVM: nothing in the bb classpath ever requires a jolt.kmet.*
 ;; namespace (kmet's guarded requires check for *jolt-version* first).
 :jolt/min-version "0.8.0"

 :paths ["src"]

 ;; The classes this lib installs (RFC 0014). jolt autoloads the install
 ;; namespace on the FIRST reference to one of these, whatever the registry
 ;; state (a claim is authority over who implements a class, not a fallback
 ;; for an absent one). java.util.Base64 is
 ;; deliberately absent: the runtime implements the class, so a claim would
 ;; be refused — getMimeDecoder is added at install, and the guarded
 ;; requires in kmet.libs.crypto / kmet.ai.google-adc are what install it
 ;; before a referencing namespace is analyzed. The asymmetric classes are
 ;; absent too: jolt.crypto provides Signature/KeyPairGenerator/KeyFactory
 ;; (EC and RSA) itself, and jolt allows a class only one provider.
 :jolt/provides {jolt.kmet.providers ["java.net.http.HttpTimeoutException"]}}
